# vbx signing configuration — TEMPLATE
#
# Copy to `scripts/signing.env` and fill in. That file is gitignored and must
# stay that way: this repository is public, and every value below identifies
# an Apple developer account.
#
#     cp scripts/signing.env.example scripts/signing.env
#     $EDITOR scripts/signing.env
#
# Everything here can also be supplied as an environment variable, which is
# what CI should do — a secret store, never a file in the checkout. The
# environment wins over the file, so a CI run needs no file at all.
#
# `./scripts/package-app.sh --check` validates whatever it ends up with, and
# masks the values in everything it prints.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Common
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Your 10-character Apple Developer Team ID, from
# https://developer.apple.com/account under Membership details.
#
# This is the value most likely to end up somewhere public by accident: it is
# embedded in certificate names, printed by `codesign -dvvv`, and required
# verbatim in App Store entitlements. Everything in this repo that needs it
# either reads it from here or generates the file that carries it into
# `.build/`, which is ignored.
VBX_TEAM_ID=ABCDE12345

# The bundle identifier to sign. Only change this if you are shipping a fork
# under your own identifier — it must match the App ID registered in your
# developer account.
VBX_BUNDLE_ID=com.qjam.vbx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Developer ID — direct distribution as a .dmg
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The certificate's full common name, exactly as `security find-identity -v -p
# codesigning` prints it. Quoting matters; the name contains spaces.
VBX_DEVELOPER_ID_APP="Developer ID Application: Your Name (ABCDE12345)"

# The notarytool keychain profile holding your Apple ID and an app-specific
# password. Create it once, interactively — it is deliberately not scripted,
# because it takes a credential that must never reach a build script:
#
#     xcrun notarytool store-credentials "vbx-notary" \
#         --apple-id you@example.com \
#         --team-id ABCDE12345 \
#         --password <app-specific-password>
#
# The profile *name* is local and not secret; the credential it stores lives in
# the login keychain and never appears in this file.
VBX_NOTARY_PROFILE=vbx-notary

# --- or an App Store Connect API key, which is preferred -------------------
#
# Set these three and VBX_NOTARY_PROFILE is not needed. This is the better form
# for two reasons: it is the same credential that creates the Developer ID
# certificate (see scripts/signing-setup.sh), so there is one thing to obtain
# and rotate rather than two; and it is the only form that works anywhere but a
# developer's own machine, because a keychain profile cannot travel to CI.
#
#   appstoreconnect.apple.com -> Users and Access -> Integrations
#     -> App Store Connect API -> generate a Team key and download the .p8
#
# Creating a *Developer ID* certificate needs the Account Holder role; an Admin
# key can notarize but cannot create one.
#
# The .p8 is the private key. Keep it outside this repository — the path here is
# a path, not the key — and readable only by you.
#VBX_NOTARY_KEY=~/.vbx-signing/AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8
#VBX_NOTARY_KEY_ID=XXXXXXXXXX
#VBX_NOTARY_ISSUER=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mac App Store
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# App Store submissions use a different pair of certificates: one signs the
# app, the other signs the .pkg that gets uploaded.
VBX_APP_STORE_APP="Apple Distribution: Your Name (ABCDE12345)"
VBX_APP_STORE_INSTALLER="3rd Party Mac Developer Installer: Your Name (ABCDE12345)"

# Path to a Mac App Store provisioning profile downloaded from your developer
# account. It is copied into the bundle as `Contents/embedded.provisionprofile`,
# which the App Store requires. Keep it outside the repository — it carries
# your team identifier and the certificates it is bound to.
VBX_PROVISION_PROFILE="$HOME/Library/MobileDevice/Provisioning Profiles/vbx-mas.provisionprofile"
